Ladybird’s permissions.remove() refuses to remove a required permission,
and an origin that overlaps a required host pattern or a manifest
content script’s match, with “You cannot remove required permissions.”
and changes nothing. That’s Chromium’s PermissionsRemoveFunction::
 Run(), which classifies the requested origins against the required
explicit and scriptable hosts first. Gecko’s ext-permissions.js
refuses only origins a required one subsumes, and only under an
enterprise policy, and WebKit’s WebExtensionContext::permissionsRemove()
removes whatever matches.

With no prompt yet, Ladybird’s permissions.request() resolves with
false, the answer a user declining gives in Chromium, Gecko, and WebKit.
